window.docContents[206].resolve({"/#reader-io-custom-monad":{"contents":"While this implementation of doug works, manually passing the configuration around is verbose and error-prone.\nThe type system will not catch it if the wrong configuration is passed downwards, for instance.\nA reader effect ensures that the same configuration is passed to all recursive calls, unless it is manually overridden, and it helps make the code less verbose.\n\nTo create a version of  that is also a reader of , first define the type and its  instance, following the recipe from the evaluator example:\n\n\n\nThe difference between this  instance and the one for  is that this one uses do-notation in the  monad as the body of the function that  returns, rather than applying  directly to the value returned from .\nAny  effects performed by  must occur before  is invoked, which is ensured by the  monad's  operator.\n is not universe polymorphic because the underlying  type is also not universe polymorphic.\n\nRunning a  action involves transforming it into an  action by providing it with a configuration:\n\n\n\nThis function is not really necessary, as a caller could simply provide the configuration directly.\nHowever, naming the operation can make it easier to see which parts of the code are intended to run in which monad.\n\nThe next step is to define a means of accessing the current configuration as part of :\n\n\n\nThis is just like  from the evaluator example, except it uses 's  to return its value rather than doing so directly.\nBecause entering a directory modifies the current configuration for the scope of a recursive call, it will be necessary to have a way to override a configuration:\n\n\n\nMuch of the code used in doug has no need for configurations, and doug calls ordinary Lean  actions from the standard library that certainly don't need a .\nOrdinary  actions can be run using , which ignores the configuration argument:\n\n\n\nWith these components,  and  can be updated to take their configuration arguments implicitly through the  monad.\nThey use nested actions to retrieve the configuration, and  to actually execute the call to :\n\n\n\nIn the new version of , the calls to  and  are wrapped in .\nAdditionally, instead of building a new configuration and then requiring the programmer to keep track of which one to pass to recursive calls, it uses  to naturally delimit the modified configuration to only a small region of the program, in which it is the only valid configuration:\n\n\n\nThe new version of  uses  to invoke  with the initial configuration:\n\n\n\nThis custom monad has a number of advantages over passing configurations manually:\n\n1. It is easier to ensure that configurations are passed down unchanged, except when changes are desired2. The concern of passing the configuration onwards is more clearly separated from the concern of printing directory contents3. As the program grows, there will be more and more intermediate layers that do nothing with configurations except propagate them, and these layers don't need to be rewritten as the configuration logic changes\n\nHowever, there are also some clear downsides:\n\n1. As the program evolves and the monad requires more features, each of the basic operators such as  and  will need to be updated2. Wrapping ordinary  actions in  is noisy and distracts from the flow of the program3. Writing monads instances by hand is repetitive, and the technique for adding a reader effect to another monad is a design pattern that requires documentation and communication overhead\n\nUsing a technique called monad transformers, all of these downsides can be addressed.\nA monad transformer takes a monad as an argument and returns a new monad.\nMonad transformers consist of:\n\n1. A definition of the transformer itself, which is typically a function from types to types2. A  instance that assumes the inner type is already a monad3. An operator to “lift” an action from the inner monad to the transformed monad, akin to \n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers\u0009Combining IO and Reader","header":"6.1.2. Using a Custom Monad","id":"/#reader-io-custom-monad"}});